    bpa.c:245 bpa_static_conflict: it turns bpa_static_clash()'s answer into
                                   the sentence an operator reads, which needs
                                   VPP's format() and a clib_error_t. WHICH of
                                   the two clashes it is - and that a
                                   difference of protocol or of public address
                                   is not one - is the decision, and
                                   test/test_bpa_static.c drives that. The
                                   widths of both sentences are read by
                                   test/test_prismash_refusals.py, and
                                   lab/forward.sh produces them on a box. Two
                                   forwards that cannot both hold. One public
                                   SOCKET serves one subscriber socket, and one
                                   subscriber socket answers on one public
                                   socket - the second is endpoint-independent
                                   mapping (RFC 4787 REQ-1), which the rest of
                                   this box already guarantees and a second
                                   forward on the same socket would quietly
                                   break. The public socket is (address, port).
                                   A pool has as many addresses as the operator
                                   gave it and hands the same port offset to
                                   one subscriber per address, so
                                   203.0.113.16:1100 and 203.0.113.17:1100 are
                                   two sockets that two customers already own.
                                   Comparing the port alone made a port
                                   sellable once per POOL instead of once per
                                   address, and refused the second customer by
                                   naming a first one they have never heard of
                                   (issue #214). The private side is
                                   deliberately NOT narrowed the same way: a
                                   subscriber's public address is derived from
                                   its private one, so (ip_priv, port_priv) is
                                   already one address's worth of socket. Both
                                   callers run bpa_static_validate() first,
                                   which is where ip_pub is derived. A forward
                                   that has not been through it has no address
                                   to compare and must not reach here.
    bpa.c:282 bpa_static_resolve_say: it words bpa_nat64_static_resolve()'s
                                      answer with format().
    bpa.c:307 bpa_statics_validate: it words the refusal bpa_statics_complete()
                                    found, through bpa_static_validate() and
                                    bpa_static_conflict(), which need VPP's
                                    format() and a clib_error_t. Which forward
                                    fails, and that each is judged against the
                                    ones before it and written back completed,
                                    is bpa_statics_complete() in bpa_pool.h,
                                    driven by test/test_bpa_pool.c.
    bpa.c:358 bpa_static_pin_site: it asks bpa_main which worker owns a
                                   subscriber's block, through bpa_sub_thread()
                                   and bpa_worker_id(), neither of which has
                                   meaning outside a running VPP with workers.
                                   lab/dynamic-workers.sh drives a pinned
                                   forward on a box with several.
    bpa.c:377 bpa_static_dyn: it reaches into bpa_main's per-thread state for
                              one worker's dynamic allocator. The allocator
                              itself is bpa_dyn.c and test/test_bpa_dyn.c
                              drives it.
    bpa.c:409 bpa_static_holder: it asks that allocator who is holding the
                                 block a forward would pin, which needs the
                                 per-worker state above. The refusal it words
                                 is produced on a box by lab/dynamic.sh.
    bpa.c:462 bpa_static_pin: it takes the worker barrier and pins or releases
                              a block in one worker's allocator. bpa_dyn_pin()
                              and bpa_dyn_unpin() are the arithmetic and
                              test/test_bpa_dyn.c drives them.
    bpa.c:532 bpa_log_pins_replay: it takes the worker barrier and walks every
                                   pool in bpa_main to re-announce the blocks
                                   forwards are holding. What a re-announcement
                                   looks like is read on a box by lab/ipfix.sh.
    bpa.c:590 bpa_static_only_on_port: it words the refusal, which needs VPP's
                                       format() and a clib_error_t - and the
                                       sentence names the first two
                                       subscribers, so it needs them and not
                                       only a count. HOW MANY forwards a
                                       protocol and a public port have, and
                                       which two to name, is
                                       bpa_static_on_port() in bpa_pool.h and
                                       test/test_bpa_static.c drives it.
                                       lab/forward.sh produces this refusal on
                                       a box. The one forward on (protocol,
                                       public port) when the caller did not say
                                       which subscriber, or an error naming the
                                       ones it could have meant. The public
                                       address is derived and never typed, so
                                       the public port used to BE the name of a
                                       forward. On a pool with several public
                                       addresses it names up to one forward per
                                       address, and removing the first match
                                       would take an inbound port away from a
                                       customer nobody mentioned. Ambiguity is
                                       refused and the operator is told how to
                                       say which - the subscriber, which is the
                                       one thing they do type.
    bpa.c:636 bpa_static_add_del: it looks the pool up in bpa_main's hash,
                                  mutates the vector under the worker barrier
                                  and pins or releases a block. The decisions
                                  it takes are bpa_static_position(),
                                  bpa_static_on_port() and the two above, all
                                  in bpa_pool.h; lab/forward.sh drives the
                                  whole thing on a box.
    bpa.c:769 bpa_check_pool_overlap: it walks bpa_main's pool of pools with
                                      pool_foreach and formats a clib_error_t.
                                      Whether two pools collide - which side,
                                      and when the VRFs keep them apart - is
                                      bpa_pools_collide() in bpa_pool.h, which
                                      test/test_bpa_pool.c drives (#424, #472);
                                      what is left here is the walk and the
                                      sentence, and lab/two-pools.sh produces
                                      both on a box.
    bpa.c:823 bpa_pool_validate: it is the order the checks run in, and two of
                                 them walk VPP's own pools
                                 (bpa_check_local_overlap,
                                 bpa_check_pool_overlap). The order is the
                                 finding it carries - the forwards last,
                                 because every reason to refuse one is a fact
                                 bpa_pool_init() computes - and
                                 lab/two-pools.sh and lab/forward.sh produce it
                                 on a box.
    bpa.c:880 bpa_outside_window_update: it walks bpa_main's pools; the
                                         window's arithmetic is
                                         bpa_window_widen() and
                                         bpa_window_close() in bpa_pool.h,
                                         driven by test/test_bpa_pool.c. The
                                         out2in path checks a packet against it
                                         before looking anything up, and
                                         lab/datapath.sh drives that path.
    bpa.c:911 bpa_interface_cgnat_mtu: it asks vnet whether a CGNAT feature is
                                       enabled on an interface and what that
                                       interface's IP4 MTU is - four
                                       vnet_feature_is_enabled() calls and
                                       vnet_sw_interface_get_mtu(). lab/mss.sh
                                       is where an MTU reaches a subscriber's
                                       TCP handshake.
    bpa.c:937 bpa_auto_mss_slots: it grows two VPP vectors on bpa_main so they
                                  can be indexed by sw_if_index. What they hold
                                  is decided by bpa_auto_mss_storage() in
                                  bpa_auto_mss.h, which
                                  test/test_bpa_auto_mss.c drives.
    bpa.c:953 bpa_auto_mss_interface_update: it reads an interface's MTU out of
                                             vnet and writes the clamp into
                                             those vectors. The arithmetic -
                                             what MSS an MTU allows - is
                                             bpa_auto_mss.h's and is driven by
                                             test/test_bpa_auto_mss.c.
    bpa.c:984 bpa_auto_mss_update: the same for every interface this box has,
                                   which means walking vnet's interface pool.
                                   lab/mss.sh reads the clamp off a handshake.
    bpa.c:1050 bpa_rx_align: it reads and moves VPP's queue placement, which
                             only a running dataplane has; bpa_rx_thread_for()
                             is the choice, held by test/test_bpa_workers.c,
                             and the #475 bench measured the handoff it
                             removes.
    bpa.c:1094 bpa_interface_feature_enable_disable: it enables or disables
                                                     this plugin's nodes on an
                                                     interface through
                                                     vnet_feature_enable_disable(),
                                                     which is VPP's graph.
                                                     test/test_feature_order.py
                                                     holds the order those
                                                     nodes sit in, and
                                                     lab/datapath.sh sends
                                                     packets through them.
    bpa.c:1212 bpa_floor_warn: it formats one RFC floor's warning with VPP's
                               format() and writes it to a vlib_main_t. WHICH
                               floors exist and what each breaks is the X-macro
                               in bpa_pool.h, which test/test_doc_copies.py
                               reads against the three places those numbers are
                               written down.
    bpa.c:1244 bpa_timeouts_warn: it walks that X-macro calling the one above,
                                  so it needs the same vlib_main_t, and the
                                  numbers it walks are held by
                                  test/test_doc_copies.py.
    bpa.c:1299 bpa_stride_short: it reads how many workers this box has out of
                                 bpa_main, which exists only inside VPP. THE
                                 ARITHMETIC is bpa_stride_short_at() in
                                 bpa_pool.h and test/test_bpa_pool.c drives it;
                                 what is here is the floor - one worker on a
                                 worker-less box - kept in the one place that
                                 reads the count rather than at each of the
                                 three callers.
    bpa.c:1329 bpa_stride_say: it prints the stride warning to a vlib_main_t's
                               CLI, which is `show bpa pool`'s screen. WHETHER
                               to warn is bpa_stride_short_at() in bpa_pool.h
                               and is driven there; the words are read by
                               test/test_plugin_cli_says.py, and
                               lab/crossaddr.sh drives a strided pool on a box.
    bpa.c:1362 bpa_stride_warn: the same sentence at the moment the stride is
                                set, written to vlib's log rather than the CLI.
                                The decision is bpa_stride_short_at().
    bpa.c:1415 bpa_reserve_short: the same reading, out of the same bpa_main.
                                  The arithmetic is bpa_reserve_short_at() in
                                  bpa_pool.h and is driven there.
    bpa.c:1442 bpa_reserve_say: it prints the reserve warning to a
                                vlib_main_t's CLI. The decision is
                                bpa_reserve_short_at() in bpa_pool.h, driven
                                there.
    bpa.c:1467 bpa_reserve_warn: the same sentence at the moment the reserve is
                                 set, to vlib's log. The decision is
                                 bpa_reserve_short_at().
    bpa.c:1486 bpa_pool_settings_set: it finds the pool in bpa_main and
                                      rewrites the settings under the barrier,
                                      then re-derives what depends on them. The
                                      refusals it applies are shared with
                                      bpa_pool_init() by construction (#375),
                                      and lab/vty-pool.sh sets every one of
                                      them on a box.
    bpa.c:1583 bpa_bypass_set: it builds an address set and stores it on a pool
                               in bpa_main. The set builder is
                               bpa_addr_set_build() in bpa_pool.h, which
                               test/test_bpa_pool.c drives; lab/bypass.sh sends
                               packets past it.
    bpa.c:1630 bpa_limits_set: it stores two numbers on a pool in bpa_main.
                               What they do is measured on a box by
                               lab/limits.sh, which fills a pool to its session
                               limit and reads the refusal.
    bpa.c:1662 bpa_nat64_check_overlap: it walks bpa_main's pools; the overlap
                                        rule is bpa_ip6_prefixes_overlap(),
                                        which test/test_bpa_nat64.c holds.
    bpa.c:1701 bpa_pool_add: it inserts a pool into bpa_main's pool and hash
                             under the worker barrier. What it validates with
                             is bpa_pool_validate() above and bpa_pool_init(),
                             which test/test_bpa_pool.c drives against
                             ref/bpa_model.py.
    bpa.c:1934 bpa_barrier_yield: it spins on vlib_time_now() to let workers
                                  past the barrier, which is VPP's clock and
                                  VPP's barrier. What it protects is measured
                                  by lab/pool-del.sh, which deletes a pool
                                  under traffic.
    bpa.c:1991 bpa_pool_log_blocks: it walks one pool's blocks under the worker
                                    barrier and queues a record for each. What
                                    a record contains is
                                    test/test_bpa_log_syslog.c's and
                                    test/test_bpa_audit.c's; lab/ipfix.sh reads
                                    them off a collector.
    bpa.c:2082 bpa_pool_del: it takes the worker barrier, drains the pool's
                             blocks and removes it from bpa_main.
                             lab/pool-del.sh is where a pool is deleted on a
                             box and the sessions that were on it are read.
    bpa.c:2189 bpa_blocks_log_all: the same for every pool, at the moment
                                   logging is switched on. lab/ipfix.sh drives
                                   that switch on a box.
    bpa.c:2281 bpa_drain_blocks: it walks a pool's blocks releasing them, under
                                 the barrier, so the log closes every
                                 allocation it opened. lab/ipfix-stop.sh
                                 measures the drain on a box and
                                 lab/drain-slope.sh times it.
    bpa.c:2315 bpa_stop_drain: the CLI's door to that drain, and the one
                               vpp-stop-drain.conf runs on the way down.
                               test/test_bpa_dyn.c drives the allocator it
                               empties; lab/ipfix-stop.sh reads the records it
                               writes.
    bpa.c:2430 bpa_ip4_address_change: a VPP callback: the stack calls it when
                                       an address is added to or removed from
                                       an interface, with an
                                       ip4_main_t-registered function pointer
                                       that exists nowhere else.
                                       lab/localaddr.sh is where a box's own
                                       address meets a pool.
    bpa.c:2471 bpa_local_rebuild: it walks VPP's interface-address pool and
                                  takes the worker barrier, which only a
                                  running dataplane has; the set it builds is
                                  bpa_local_build(), which
                                  test/test_bpa_local.c holds.
    bpa.c:2505 bpa_local_address_change: VPP's interface-address callback,
                                         which only passes its arguments to
                                         bpa_local_rebuild().
    bpa.c:2517 bpa_init: the plugin's init, called once by VPP with the graph
                         up.
    bpa.c:2655 bpa_set_owners: it resets bpa_main's worker tables from VPP's
                               worker count; the table is bpa_thread_table() in
                               bpa_workers.h, which test/test_bpa_workers.c
                               holds (#472), and lab/cgnat-workers.sh starts a
                               dataplane with a split and sends traffic through
                               it.
    bpa.c:2675 bpa_config: a VLIB_CONFIG_FUNCTION, called by VPP with its own
                           parser while it reads startup.conf;
                           lab/cgnat-workers.sh gives it one.
    bpa.h:500 bpa_pool_by_inside: it walks VPP's pool of pools; the rule for
                                  one pool is bpa_pool_claims_inside() in
                                  bpa_pool.h, held by test/test_bpa_pool.c
                                  (#472)
    bpa.h:527 bpa_maybe_outside: it reads bpa_main_t, VPP's; the rule is
                                 bpa_in_window() in bpa_pool.h, held by
                                 test/test_bpa_pool.c (#472)
    bpa.h:536 bpa_pool_by_outside: it walks VPP's pool of pools; the rule for
                                   one pool is bpa_pool_claims_outside() in
                                   bpa_pool.h, held by test/test_bpa_pool.c
                                   (#472)
    bpa.h:558 bpa_worker_id: it reads bpa_main_t, VPP's; the rule is
                             bpa_thread_worker() in bpa_workers.h, which
                             test/test_bpa_workers.c holds (#472)
    bpa.h:574 bpa_sub_thread: it reads bpa_main_t, VPP's; the rule is
                              bpa_owner_thread() in bpa_workers.h, which
                              test/test_bpa_workers.c holds (#472)
    bpa.h:646 bpa_to_us: the answer is VPP's FIB - a forwarding lookup and the
                         DPO it lands on - which no stand-in has; lab/to-us.sh
                         sends a subscriber's ping to the box itself through
                         the CGNAT and reads it answered (#470)
    bpa.h:661 bpa_next_after: the next node is VPP's feature arc
                              (vnet_feature_next_u16) or its lookup node; every
                              datapath lab takes one branch or the other
    bpa_alg.h:71 bpa_alg_splice: the room check is VPP's buffer size and the
                                 TCP checksum is VPP's own over the buffer; the
                                 rule it keeps is bpa_seq_note(), held by
                                 test/test_bpa_ftp.c, and lab/rtsp.sh reads the
                                 spliced packet (#472)
    bpa_alg.h:118 bpa_rtsp_in2out: reserves in VPP's session pool and bihash;
                                   its parts are bpa_tcp_payload(),
                                   bpa_rtsp_client_port() and
                                   bpa_rtsp_format_pair(), each held by name,
                                   and lab/rtsp.sh carries a SETUP through it
                                   (#472)
    bpa_alg.h:193 bpa_rtsp_out2in: looks the pair up in VPP's bihash; the rule
                                   it applies is bpa_rtsp_pair_is_ours(), held
                                   by test/test_bpa_rtsp.c, and lab/rtsp.sh
                                   carries the 200 back through it (#472)
    bpa_alg.h:263 bpa_alg_udp_relength: the checksum is VPP's own over the
                                        buffer; the rule it adds is
                                        bpa_udp_csum_on_wire(), held by
                                        test/test_bpa_l4.c, and lab/sip.sh
                                        checks the datagram that leaves (#472)
    bpa_alg.h:316 bpa_sip_in2out: reserves in VPP's session pool and bihash;
                                  its parts are bpa_sip_parse(),
                                  bpa_sip_growth(), the edit list and the
                                  formatters, each held by test/test_bpa_sip.c,
                                  and lab/sip.sh carries an INVITE through it
                                  (#472)
    bpa_alg.h:510 bpa_sip_out2in: rewrites a VPP buffer; the rule it applies is
                                  bpa_sip_our_via(), held by
                                  test/test_bpa_sip.c, and lab/sip.sh carries
                                  the response back through it (#472)
