    bpa_log.c:44 bpa_log_seg_create: it makes a POSIX shared-memory segment and
                                     maps it into VPP, which a unit cannot
                                     stand in for; the layout it writes is
                                     bpa_log_seg_init(), which
                                     test/test_bpa_log_ring.c drives with a
                                     real /dev/shm segment and a reader in
                                     another process. The block-event rings,
                                     one per vlib main, in
                                     /dev/shm/prisma-bpa-log (#550). A fresh
                                     segment at every start: the previous one
                                     is unlinked first, so a reader still
                                     mapping it keeps its own copy and sees the
                                     new one by its generation. Pages are
                                     touched here, on the main thread at start,
                                     so that no worker takes a page fault on
                                     its first event. If the segment cannot be
                                     had, the rings go to the main heap as they
                                     were in 0.149.7 - logging still works
                                     through VPP, and `show cgnat logging` says
                                     why nothing outside it can read them.
